                    Step 3: Sort the lines

Use the sort command:

sort data.txt

sort arranges the lines in alphabetical/numerical order.

This makes identical lines appear next to each other.

                    Step 4: Remove duplicate lines
 
We can use uniq after sorting:

sort data.txt | uniq

However, the goal is to find the line that appears only once.

So we use:

uniq -u

The -u option means unique — display only lines that occur exactly once.

                       Step 5: Use the complete command

Your screenshot shows:

cat data.txt | sort | uniq -u

This performs three operations:

cat data.txt
      ↓
Displays the file
      ↓
sort
      ↓
Sorts all lines
      ↓
uniq -u
      ↓
Finds the line occurring only once
                       Step 6: Understand the pipe |

The | symbol is called a pipe.

For example:

cat data.txt | sort

means:

Take the output of cat data.txt and send it as input to sort.

Similarly:

sort | uniq -u

means:

Send the sorted lines to uniq -u.

So the complete command connects three commands together.

             Step 7: Get the unique line

The command:

cat data.txt | sort | uniq -u

returns a single line in your screenshot. That unique line is the required password for the next level.

